# `to_char(n)`: the int -> char direction, and `'\xNN'`: the byte written in hex.
#
# WHY to_char EXISTS. `chr(n)` (src/tychoc.c:4912) already turns an int into a
# byte, but it returns that byte as a one-character STRING, so the only way to
# reach a `char` from an int was `char_at(chr(n), 0)` -- two builtins and a heap
# string for a value that is one byte. `char` has no type keyword
# (docs/spec/03-types.md:75), so nothing about this could be written as an
# annotation either; the type is inferred here exactly as it is from `char_at`.
#
# OUT OF RANGE ABORTS, it does not wrap. to_char routes through the same
# `tycho_chr` (runtime/tycho_rt.c:1193-1198) as `chr`, whose comment states the
# rule: "A value outside 0..255 is a program error and aborts cleanly (like a
# bad index), rather than silently masking to a byte." tests/abort/chr_oob.ty
# pins the abort itself. The sized `to_u8` family wraps instead, but those are
# documented as TOTAL reinterpretations (docs/spec/06-conversions.md:40), a
# different category from a conversion with a domain.
#
# `'\xNN'` takes exactly two hex digits -- deliberately fixed-width, not C's
# greedy form, and it exists in char literals only. See tests/char_hex_escape.ty.
fn main():
    a := to_char(65)
    println(str(a) + "," + str(a == 'A') + "," + str(to_int(a)))

    # the whole byte range is reachable, including 0 and 255
    println(str(to_char(0) == '\0') + "," + str(to_char(255) == '\xff'))

    # char is what `string + char` appends as a glyph, so no chr() detour
    s := ""
    for i := 72; i < 76; i += 1:
        s = s + to_char(i)
    println(s)

    # round-trip against the two spellings it replaces
    println(str(to_char(97) == char_at(chr(97), 0)) + "," + str(chr(97) == str(to_char(97))))
